cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this study or its code, please cite it as below."
title: "Why Are HPC Monitoring Signals Hard to Forecast? A Predictability Study on M100 ExaData"
abstract: >-
  A study of the intrinsic predictability of high-performance-computing
  monitoring telemetry, using the public M100 ExaData release from CINECA's
  Marconi100 Tier-0 supercomputer. Rather than treating poor forecast accuracy
  as a modelling deficiency, the study measures model-free predictability
  properties of each signal — entropy rate, periodicity, long memory,
  non-stationarity, quantisation, sampling irregularity, and exogeneity — and
  tests whether they explain the forecast skill actually achieved by a tiered
  suite of forecasters ranging from naive baselines to neural models. Signals
  are drawn from all nine ExaData plugin families, so signal physics varies
  while the machine, workload, and observation period are held fixed.
